DATA.eventsFull = [{"id":"moctezuma-accession","t":1502.7041,"name":"Moctezuma II becomes huey tlatoani","kind":"political","precision":"year","lon":-99.1313,"lat":19.4348,"confidence":"moderate","facts":[["Date (Julian)","15 Sep 1502 (year-level date)"],["Gregorian","25 Sep 1502"]],"text":"Moctezuma Xocoyotzin succeeds Ahuítzotl as huey tlatoani of the Triple Alliance. The empire he inherits takes tribute from some 38 provinces but rules through local dynasts, not garrisons — a network of obligations, not a surface.","accounts":[],"sources":["[CM] Codex Mendoza","[DUR]","[TH]"],"track":false,"pins":null},{"id":"new-fire-1507","t":1507.8712,"name":"The last New Fire ceremony","kind":"religious","precision":"year","lon":-99.0897,"lat":19.3363,"confidence":"good","facts":[["Date (Julian)","15 Nov 1507 (year-level date)"],["Gregorian","25 Nov 1507"]],"text":"On Huixachtlan (Cerro de la Estrella) the priests bind the 52-year cycle for what will prove the last time. The ceremony asserts the world's continuation under Mexica stewardship.","accounts":[],"sources":["[FC] Bk VII","[DUR]"],"track":false,"pins":null},{"id":"omens-reported","t":1509.4137,"name":"The omens later remembered","kind":"religious","precision":"year","lon":-99.1313,"lat":19.4348,"confidence":"contested","facts":[["Date (Julian)","1 Jun 1509 (year-level date)"],["Gregorian","11 Jun 1509"]],"text":"A comet by day, the temple that burned unquenchably, the lake that boiled without wind, the weeping woman in the night — Book XII opens with eight omens said to have troubled the empire's last decade. Whether anyone saw them before 1519 is exactly the question.","accounts":[{"source":"Florentine Codex Bk XII (c. 1555-79)","claim":"eight portents foretold the empire's fall, and Moctezuma read them with dread","note":"compiled a generation AFTER the fall — prophecy is easiest in retrospect"},{"source":"modern scholarship (Restall 2018; Townsend 2019)","claim":"the omen list is a post-conquest frame, shaped by both Nahua fatalism-in-defeat and European portent conventions","note":"the model shows the omens as MEMORY, not as events of 1509"}],"sources":["[FC] Bk XII ch.1","[DUR]","Restall (2018)"],"track":false,"pins":null},{"id":"tlachquiauhco-1511","t":1511.4137,"name":"The empire's last expansion: Tlachquiauhco","kind":"campaign","precision":"year","lon":-97.68,"lat":17.271,"confidence":"moderate","facts":[["Date (Julian)","1 Jun 1511 (year-level date)"],["Gregorian","11 Jun 1511"]],"text":"Moctezuma II's armies take Tlachquiauhco in the Mixteca Alta — the tribute system's final addition, eight years before Cortés lands.","accounts":[],"sources":["[CM] conquest folios","[HAS]"],"track":false,"pins":null},{"id":"nezahualpilli-dies","t":1515.4137,"name":"Nezahualpilli of Texcoco dies; the succession splits","kind":"political","precision":"year","lon":-98.883,"lat":19.508,"confidence":"moderate","facts":[["Date (Julian)","1 Jun 1515 (year-level date)"],["Gregorian","11 Jun 1515"]],"text":"Moctezuma backs Cacama for the Acolhua throne; his half-brother Ixtlilxóchitl refuses the result and holds the northern Acolhua highlands in arms. The alliance's second seat is fractured four years before any Spaniard arrives.","accounts":[{"source":"Alva Ixtlilxóchitl (c. 1610-40)","claim":"Ixtlilxóchitl held the northern provinces and later delivered half the empire to the coalition","note":"the chronicler is the rebel's great-great-grandson, writing to secure his family's standing"},{"source":"Cortés, Cartas; Bernal Díaz","claim":"the Spanish accounts barely register the Acolhua split until 1520","note":"they had no view into alliance politics before Texcoco changed sides"}],"sources":["[IXT]","[TH]"],"track":false,"pins":null},{"id":"cordoba-1517","t":1517.1616,"name":"Córdoba's expedition touches Yucatán","kind":"campaign","precision":"month","lon":-90.72,"lat":19.35,"confidence":"moderate","facts":[["Date (Julian)","1 Mar 1517 (month approximate)"],["Gregorian","11 Mar 1517"]],"text":"The first Spanish expedition from Cuba is mauled at Champotón; word of bearded strangers on the coast begins to travel the trade roads.","accounts":[],"sources":["[BD]","[TH]"],"track":false,"pins":null},{"id":"grijalva-1518","t":1518.4137,"name":"Grijalva coasts the Gulf; first embassies","kind":"campaign","precision":"month","lon":-95.76,"lat":18.78,"confidence":"moderate","facts":[["Date (Julian)","1 Jun 1518 (month approximate)"],["Gregorian","11 Jun 1518"]],"text":"Grijalva trades along the Gulf coast. Moctezuma's stewards meet the ships and carry reports and gifts to Tenochtitlan.","accounts":[],"sources":["[BD]","[FC]","[TH]"],"track":false,"pins":null},{"id":"depart-cuba","t":1519.1315,"name":"The company leaves Cuba","kind":"campaign","precision":"day","lon":-84.9,"lat":21.9,"confidence":"moderate","facts":[["Date (Julian)","18 Feb 1519"],["Gregorian","28 Feb 1519"],["Nahua day (correlation)","4 Técpatl — a correlation, not an attestation"]],"text":"Cortés sails from Cuba with ~500-630 men, ~16 horses and ~11 ships, in defiance of governor Velázquez's revocation — the expedition is illegal from its first day, which shapes every letter Cortés will write about it.","accounts":[],"sources":["[BD]","[C2]","[TH]"],"track":true,"pins":null},{"id":"centla","t":1519.2274,"name":"Battle of Centla","kind":"battle","precision":"day","lon":-92.65,"lat":18.28,"confidence":"moderate","facts":[["Date (Julian)","25 Mar 1519"],["Gregorian","4 Apr 1519"],["Nahua day (correlation)","13 Ácatl — a correlation, not an attestation"]],"text":"The company defeats the Chontal Maya of Potonchan; among the tribute of peace is a group of enslaved women including Malintzin, who will become the expedition's Nahuatl-Maya interpreter and political instrument.","accounts":[],"sources":["[BD]","[GOM]","[TH]"],"track":true,"pins":null},{"id":"ulua-landing","t":1519.3014,"name":"Landing at San Juan de Ulúa","kind":"campaign","precision":"day","lon":-96.13,"lat":19.21,"confidence":"good","facts":[["Date (Julian)","21 Apr 1519"],["Gregorian","1 May 1519"],["Nahua day (correlation)","1 Xóchitl — a correlation, not an attestation"]],"text":"The fleet anchors off the dunes opposite San Juan de Ulúa, inside the tribute province of Cuetlaxtlan. Moctezuma's governors arrive within days with gifts and painters to record the strangers.","accounts":[],"sources":["[BD]","[C2]","[FC]","[TH]"],"track":true,"pins":{"[BD]":"cap. 36-40"}},{"id":"villa-rica-founded","t":1519.4877,"name":"Villa Rica de la Vera Cruz founded; the legal coup","kind":"political","precision":"month","lon":-96.396,"lat":19.675,"confidence":"moderate","facts":[["Date (Julian)","28 Jun 1519 (month approximate)"],["Gregorian","8 Jul 1519"]],"text":"The company constitutes itself a town, elects a cabildo, and has the new town commission Cortés directly under the crown — the legal device that unhooks the expedition from Cuba. The first Totonac alliance is made the same season.","accounts":[{"source":"Cortés, Primera/Segunda carta (1519-20)","claim":"the town's commission made the enterprise lawful and royal","note":"the letters exist to argue exactly this to a crown that could hang him"},{"source":"Velázquez's party; later lawsuits","claim":"a mutiny dressed as a municipality","note":"the rival faction's framing, pursued in court for years"}],"sources":["[C2]","[BD]","[TH]"],"track":false,"pins":{"[C2]":"Second Letter, the founding of Villa Rica de la Vera Cruz","[BD]":"cap. 41-42"}},{"id":"cempoala-tribute-refusal","t":1519.5342,"name":"Cempoala seizes Moctezuma's tribute collectors","kind":"political","precision":"month","lon":-96.404,"lat":19.446,"confidence":"moderate","facts":[["Date (Julian)","15 Jul 1519 (month approximate)"],["Gregorian","25 Jul 1519"]],"text":"At Cortés's urging the Totonac towns arrest the imperial collectors — the first open breach in the tribute system, engineered so the Totonacs cannot step back. The grievance that made it possible was the tribute itself.","accounts":[],"sources":["[BD]","[C2]"],"track":false,"pins":null},{"id":"ships-scuttled","t":1519.6055,"name":"The ships are run ashore","kind":"campaign","precision":"month","lon":-96.396,"lat":19.675,"confidence":"moderate","facts":[["Date (Julian)","10 Aug 1519 (month approximate)"],["Gregorian","20 Aug 1519"]],"text":"Cortés strips and beaches the fleet: no way back to Cuba, every man committed to the march inland.","accounts":[{"source":"Cortés, Segunda carta (1520)","claim":"ships grounded as unseaworthy","note":"understates the coercion of his own Velázquez faction"},{"source":"Bernal Díaz; Gómara","claim":"deliberately destroyed to foreclose retreat","note":"the soldiers' memory of the stakes"}],"sources":["[C2]","[BD]","[GOM]"],"track":false,"pins":null},{"id":"march-inland","t":1519.6219,"name":"The march inland begins","kind":"campaign","precision":"day","lon":-96.404,"lat":19.446,"confidence":"moderate","facts":[["Date (Julian)","16 Aug 1519"],["Gregorian","26 Aug 1519"],["Nahua day (correlation)","1 Ollin — a correlation, not an attestation"]],"text":"~300-400 Spaniards with Totonac porters and warriors leave Cempoala for the highlands, by way of Xicochimalco toward Tlaxcallan.","accounts":[],"sources":["[C2]","[BD]","[TH]"],"track":true,"pins":null},{"id":"tlaxcala-battles","t":1519.6685,"name":"Tlaxcallan fights the company","kind":"battle","precision":"day","lon":-98.238,"lat":19.318,"confidence":"good","facts":[["Date (Julian)","2 Sep 1519"],["Gregorian","12 Sep 1519"],["Nahua day (correlation)","5 Océlotl — a correlation, not an attestation"]],"text":"The Tlaxcalteca under Xicohtencatl the Younger attack the column repeatedly over two weeks — the hardest indigenous fighting the Spaniards will face in open field, ended by Tlaxcallan's own council, not by defeat in rout.","accounts":[],"sources":["[C2]","[BD]","[MC]"],"track":true,"pins":{"[C2]":"Second Letter, the fighting in Tlaxcala","[BD]":"cap. 62-69"}},{"id":"tlaxcala-alliance","t":1519.726,"name":"The Tlaxcala alliance","kind":"political","precision":"day","lon":-98.238,"lat":19.318,"confidence":"good","facts":[["Date (Julian)","23 Sep 1519"],["Gregorian","3 Oct 1519"],["Nahua day (correlation)","13 Cuauhtli — a correlation, not an attestation"]],"text":"The company enters Tlaxcallan as guests. The confederation — blockaded and salt-starved by the Triple Alliance for a generation — chooses the lever: its warriors, porters and roads become the war's indigenous backbone. Without this choice there is no siege and no conquest.","accounts":[{"source":"Muñoz Camargo; the Lienzo de Tlaxcala (c. 1552)","claim":"Tlaxcallan as co-conqueror from the first day, by free decision","note":"produced while Tlaxcala petitioned the crown for the privileges that service earned"},{"source":"Cortés, Segunda carta","claim":"vassals gained for the crown by his diplomacy","note":"every ally is written as his instrument"}],"sources":["[C2]","[BD]","[MC]"],"track":false,"pins":{"[C2]":"Second Letter, the peace with Tlaxcala","[BD]":"cap. 70-77","[MC]":"Historia de Tlaxcala, lib. I"},"image":{"src":"img/cards/lienzo-meeting.jpg","caption":"The Lienzo de Tlaxcala: Cortés and the lords of Tlaxcallan — Malintzin interpreting at the centre","credit":"Lienzo de Tlaxcala (c. 1552), public domain, via Wikimedia Commons"}},{"id":"cholula-massacre","t":1519.7945,"name":"The Cholula massacre","kind":"massacre","precision":"day","lon":-98.3064,"lat":19.0633,"confidence":"contested","facts":[["Date (Julian)","18 Oct 1519"],["Gregorian","28 Oct 1519"],["Nahua day (correlation)","12 Xóchitl — a correlation, not an attestation"]],"text":"In the courtyard of the temple of Quetzalcóatl the company and its Tlaxcalteca allies kill several thousand assembled Chololteca. Whether a Mexica-backed ambush was pre-empted or an unarmed crowd was made an example of is the war's most disputed question; the toll itself is reported from ~3,000 (Cortés) upward.","accounts":[{"source":"Cortés, Segunda carta (1520)","claim":"an ambush was set; the strike pre-empted it; ~3,000 died","note":"a legal brief for an unauthorised war; the plot justifies the killing"},{"source":"Bernal Díaz (c. 1568)","claim":"confirms the plot, credits Malintzin's warning","note":"written decades later, against Gómara, by a participant with his own case"},{"source":"Florentine Codex Bk XII (c. 1555-79)","claim":"the Chololteca were assembled unarmed in the sacred courtyard and slaughtered without cause","note":"Tlaxcalteca guides had their own feud with Cholula; Tlatelolca informants a generation later"},{"source":"modern scholarship [TH][HAS]","claim":"evidence for the plot is unverifiable; the effect — terror through the region — is not in doubt","note":"the model draws the event and lets the accounts disagree"}],"sources":["[C2]","[BD]","[FC]","[MC]","[TH]"],"track":false,"pins":{"[C2]":"Second Letter, the affair at Cholula","[BD]":"cap. 83","[FC]":"Bk XII, ch. 11"},"image":{"src":"img/cards/lienzo-cholula.jpg","caption":"The Lienzo de Tlaxcala's Cholula plate — the massacre as the coalition's own artists recorded it","credit":"Lienzo de Tlaxcala (c. 1552), public domain, via Wikimedia Commons"}},{"id":"ordaz-popocatepetl","t":1519.8137,"name":"Ordaz climbs the smoking mountain","kind":"campaign","precision":"month","lon":-98.628,"lat":19.023,"confidence":"moderate","facts":[["Date (Julian)","25 Oct 1519 (month approximate)"],["Gregorian","4 Nov 1519"]],"text":"Diego de Ordaz and two companions climb Popocatépetl — in eruption through these years — to the crater's rim, for sulphur, for reconnaissance and for the astonishment of both armies. The volcano's plume stood over the whole war; the model draws it for the attested 1519-1528 active period.","accounts":[],"sources":["[C2]","[BD]"],"track":false,"pins":null},{"id":"cholula-realigned","t":1519.8329,"name":"Cholula realigned under coalition lords","kind":"political","precision":"month","lon":-98.3064,"lat":19.0633,"confidence":"moderate","facts":[["Date (Julian)","1 Nov 1519 (month approximate)"],["Gregorian","11 Nov 1519"]],"text":"New lords acceptable to Tlaxcallan and the company are installed; the holy city passes into the coalition's rear area.","accounts":[],"sources":["[C2]","[MC]"],"track":false,"pins":null},{"id":"paso-de-cortes","t":1519.8384,"name":"Over the pass between the volcanoes","kind":"campaign","precision":"day","lon":-98.647,"lat":19.087,"confidence":"moderate","facts":[["Date (Julian)","3 Nov 1519"],["Gregorian","13 Nov 1519"],["Nahua day (correlation)","2 Cozcacuauhtli — a correlation, not an attestation"]],"text":"The column crosses the saddle between Popocatépetl and Iztaccíhuatl and descends by Amaquemecan toward the lake plain — the first Spanish sight of the Basin and its cities on the water.","accounts":[],"sources":["[C2]","[BD]"],"track":true,"pins":null},{"id":"ayotzinco-halt","t":1519.8466,"name":"Down the lake edge: Ayotzinco and Iztapalapan","kind":"campaign","precision":"day","lon":-98.925,"lat":19.208,"confidence":"moderate","facts":[["Date (Julian)","6 Nov 1519"],["Gregorian","16 Nov 1519"],["Nahua day (correlation)","5 Quiáhuitl — a correlation, not an attestation"]],"text":"The column rounds the freshwater lakes by Ayotzinco and Cuitláhuac and is lodged in Iztapalapan by lords of Moctezuma's own family.","accounts":[],"sources":["[C2]","[BD]"],"track":true,"pins":null},{"id":"entry-tenochtitlan","t":1519.8521,"name":"The company enters Tenochtitlan; Moctezuma receives Cortés","kind":"political","precision":"day","lon":-99.135,"lat":19.406,"confidence":"good","facts":[["Date (Julian)","8 Nov 1519"],["Gregorian","18 Nov 1519"],["Nahua day (correlation)","7 Cipactli — a correlation, not an attestation"]],"text":"Over the Iztapalapan causeway, watched by canoe-borne crowds, the company is received on the causeway's meeting of roads by Moctezuma and lodged in the palace of Axayácatl. What Moctezuma's speech of welcome MEANT is the most contested sentence of the entire war.","accounts":[{"source":"Cortés, Segunda carta (1520)","claim":"Moctezuma acknowledged the emperor as the lord his ancestors awaited and 'donated' his realm","note":"a donation speech is precisely what legitimises an unauthorised conquest at law; addressed to the monarch who could ruin him"},{"source":"Florentine Codex Bk XII (c. 1555-79)","claim":"a courteous royal welcome in high Nahuatl rhetoric: 'you have arrived at your house'","note":"formal host-language, compiled a generation later under Franciscan supervision from Tlatelolca informants"},{"source":"modern scholarship (Restall 2018; Townsend 2019)","claim":"the 'surrender' and the 'returning god' reading are post-conquest constructions; the polite formulas were misread or repurposed","note":"the model states the dispute and does not adjudicate"}],"sources":["[C2]","[BD]","[FC]","[TH]"],"track":true,"pins":{"[C2]":"Second Letter, the entry of 8 November","[BD]":"cap. 87-88","[FC]":"Bk XII, ch. 15-16"},"image":{"src":"img/cards/moctezuma.jpg","caption":"Moctezuma II as the 17th century imagined him — a portrait of memory, not of life","credit":"attr. school of Antonio Rodríguez, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons"}},{"id":"moctezuma-seized","t":1519.8685,"name":"Moctezuma seized in his own city","kind":"political","precision":"day","lon":-99.1341,"lat":19.4346,"confidence":"contested","facts":[["Date (Julian)","14 Nov 1519"],["Gregorian","24 Nov 1519"],["Nahua day (correlation)","13 Mázatl — a correlation, not an attestation"]],"text":"Days after the entry, Cortés takes Moctezuma into custody in the Spanish quarters. For months the huey tlatoani rules as a hostage; the empire's centre is captured before its edge knows there is a war.","accounts":[{"source":"Cortés; Bernal Díaz","claim":"a bold pre-emptive seizure days after arrival (the pretext: the Nautla skirmish)","note":"the six-day timeline is Díaz's; Cortés is vague on dates"},{"source":"modern scholarship [TH][HAS]","claim":"the seizure may have come weeks later and more gradually than the heroic account implies","note":"the date is drawn at Díaz's timeline with precision marked contested"}],"sources":["[C2]","[BD]","[FC]"],"track":false,"pins":{"[C2]":"Second Letter, the seizure of Moctezuma","[BD]":"cap. 95","[FC]":"Bk XII, ch. 17"}},{"id":"quauhpopoca-burned","t":1519.9151,"name":"Quauhpopoca burned before the palace","kind":"political","precision":"month","lon":-99.1322,"lat":19.4338,"confidence":"contested","facts":[["Date (Julian)","1 Dec 1519 (month approximate)"],["Gregorian","11 Dec 1519"]],"text":"The lord of Nautla, whose men had killed Spaniards on the coast, is surrendered by Moctezuma, tried by Cortés, and burned alive in front of the palace — on a pyre of the arsenal's own weapons, while the hostage emperor stands in irons to watch.","accounts":[{"source":"Cortés, Segunda carta","claim":"Quauhpopoca confessed to acting on Moctezuma's orders — proving the emperor's treachery and justifying his seizure","note":"the confession, extracted before burning, is the seizure's legal foundation"},{"source":"modern scholarship [TH][HAS]","claim":"more plausibly a coastal tribute conflict; the 'orders' confession is exactly what the occasion required","note":"the model states both readings"}],"sources":["[C2]","[BD]","[TH]"],"track":false,"pins":null},{"id":"cacama-arrested","t":1520.0383,"name":"Cacama of Texcoco arrested; Acolhua politics captured","kind":"political","precision":"month","lon":-98.883,"lat":19.508,"confidence":"moderate","facts":[["Date (Julian)","15 Jan 1520 (month approximate)"],["Gregorian","25 Jan 1520"]],"text":"Cacama, planning resistance to the hostage regime, is betrayed, seized and chained; Cortés installs a pliable brother. The alliance's second seat now has three claimants and no working government.","accounts":[],"sources":["[C2]","[BD]","[IXT]"],"track":false,"pins":null},{"id":"gold-melted","t":1520.2022,"name":"The treasure of generations goes into the crucible","kind":"political","precision":"month","lon":-99.1338,"lat":19.4352,"confidence":"moderate","facts":[["Date (Julian)","15 Mar 1520 (month approximate)"],["Gregorian","25 Mar 1520"]],"text":"Axayácatl's treasure chamber — featherwork, worked gold, the accumulated art of the tributary system — is inventoried and the gold melted into portable bars with the royal fifth set aside. What the Mexica had made, the ledger unmade first.","accounts":[],"sources":["[C2]","[BD]","[FC]"],"track":false,"pins":null},{"id":"narvaez-lands","t":1520.3005,"name":"Narváez lands to arrest Cortés","kind":"campaign","precision":"day","lon":-96.13,"lat":19.21,"confidence":"moderate","facts":[["Date (Julian)","20 Apr 1520"],["Gregorian","30 Apr 1520"],["Nahua day (correlation)","2 Cóatl — a correlation, not an attestation"]],"text":"Velázquez's punitive expedition — ~900 men, the largest Spanish force yet seen on the mainland — lands at San Juan de Ulúa. With it, unrecorded by anyone at the time, travels smallpox.","accounts":[],"sources":["[BD]","[C2]","[TH]"],"track":false,"pins":null},{"id":"toxcatl","t":1520.388,"name":"The Tóxcatl massacre","kind":"massacre","precision":"day","lon":-99.131,"lat":19.4344,"confidence":"contested","facts":[["Date (Julian)","22 May 1520"],["Gregorian","1 Jun 1520"],["Nahua day (correlation)","8 Ollin — a correlation, not an attestation"]],"text":"With Cortés away on the coast, Alvarado's garrison falls on the unarmed celebrants of the feast of Tóxcatl in the sacred precinct and kills the flower of the Mexica warrior nobility. The city rises; the hostage regime is finished.","accounts":[{"source":"Alvarado's defence, via Cortés and the lawsuits","claim":"an uprising was being prepared under cover of the feast; the strike pre-empted it","note":"the perpetrator's own justification, at law"},{"source":"Florentine Codex Bk XII; Durán","claim":"unarmed dancers and drummers butchered in the courtyard, without warning or cause","note":"the Nahua accounts are unanimous and give the massacre its enduring name"}],"sources":["[FC]","[DUR]","[C2]","[BD]"],"track":false,"pins":{"[FC]":"Bk XII, ch. 20 — the fullest account, from Tlatelolca witnesses","[BD]":"cap. 125","[DUR]":"cap. 75"},"image":{"src":"img/cards/toxcatl.jpg","caption":"The Tóxcatl massacre in the walled precinct: the drum, the dancers, and Alvarado's men in the gateways","credit":"16th-c. codex image, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons"}},{"id":"cempoala-narvaez","t":1520.4044,"name":"Cortés surprises Narváez at Cempoala","kind":"battle","precision":"day","lon":-96.404,"lat":19.446,"confidence":"moderate","facts":[["Date (Julian)","28 May 1520"],["Gregorian","7 Jun 1520"],["Nahua day (correlation)","1 Calli — a correlation, not an attestation"]],"text":"A night assault takes Narváez's camp; the prisoner's army changes sides almost entire, tripling Cortés's Spanish force at exactly the moment his position in Tenochtitlan collapses.","accounts":[],"sources":["[C2]","[BD]","[GOM]"],"track":false,"pins":{"[C2]":"Second Letter, the defeat of Narváez","[BD]":"cap. 122-123"}},{"id":"cortes-returns","t":1520.4781,"name":"Cortés re-enters a risen city","kind":"campaign","precision":"day","lon":-99.1336,"lat":19.436,"confidence":"good","facts":[["Date (Julian)","24 Jun 1520"],["Gregorian","4 Jul 1520"],["Nahua day (correlation)","2 Itzcuintli — a correlation, not an attestation"]],"text":"The reinforced company re-enters Tenochtitlan unopposed — into a trap. The causeway bridges go up behind them; the palace of Axayácatl becomes a besieged island inside the island.","accounts":[],"sources":["[C2]","[BD]","[TH]"],"track":true,"pins":null},{"id":"moctezuma-dies","t":1520.4945,"name":"Death of Moctezuma II","kind":"political","precision":"day","lon":-99.1345,"lat":19.435,"confidence":"contested","facts":[["Date (Julian)","30 Jun 1520"],["Gregorian","10 Jul 1520"],["Nahua day (correlation)","8 Cozcacuauhtli — a correlation, not an attestation"]],"text":"Brought to a rooftop to calm the assault, Moctezuma is struck — by whose hand is disputed to this day — and dies in Spanish custody. With him dies the fiction that the empire's centre still functions.","accounts":[{"source":"Cortés; Bernal Díaz; Gómara","claim":"stoned by his own people while appealing for calm; died of the wounds days later, mourned by the Spaniards","note":"the version that absolves the captors, in documents written for the crown"},{"source":"Florentine Codex Bk XII; Durán; other Nahua accounts","claim":"the Spaniards killed him once his usefulness ended — some accounts specify garrotting or sword","note":"unanimous that the body the Mexica received showed the Spaniards' work; a generation later, from the other side of the siege"}],"sources":["[C2]","[BD]","[FC]","[DUR]"],"track":false,"pins":{"[C2]":"Second Letter, the death of Moctezuma","[BD]":"cap. 126","[FC]":"Bk XII, ch. 23"},"image":{"src":"img/cards/moctezuma.jpg","caption":"Moctezuma II as the 17th century imagined him — a portrait of memory, not of life","credit":"attr. school of Antonio Rodríguez, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons"}},{"id":"noche-triste","t":1520.4973,"name":"The Noche Triste: the flight from Tenochtitlan","kind":"battle","precision":"day","lon":-99.147,"lat":19.439,"confidence":"good","facts":[["Date (Julian)","1 Jul 1520"],["Gregorian","11 Jul 1520"],["Nahua day (correlation)","9 Ollin — a correlation, not an attestation"]],"text":"The company breaks out along the Tlacopan causeway in the rain at night with a portable bridge and the treasure. Caught mid-causeway by canoe assault, it loses most of its rearguard, most of the treasure, and — by Cortés's count 150, by Díaz's over 550 — its dead, plus thousands of Tlaxcalteca allies whose losses no Spanish source counts carefully.","accounts":[{"source":"Cortés, Segunda carta","claim":"~150 Spaniards and ~2,000 allies lost","note":"minimising a catastrophe in a letter asking the crown to fund its repair"},{"source":"Bernal Díaz","claim":"over 550 Spaniards lost (and 'over 860' counting the Narváez men at Tustepec)","note":"the soldiers' count, against Gómara's hero-narrative"},{"source":"Florentine Codex Bk XII; Anales de Tlatelolco","claim":"the canal of the Toltecs choked with horses, men and carried gold","note":"the Mexica memory of a victory the city did not get to keep"}],"sources":["[C2]","[BD]","[FC]","[AT]"],"track":true,"pins":{"[C2]":"Second Letter, the retreat from the city","[BD]":"cap. 128","[FC]":"Bk XII, ch. 24","[AT]":"f. 36"},"image":{"src":"img/cards/noche-triste.jpg","caption":"The Sad Night, from the 17th-century Conquest of Mexico series: the causeway fight under a crescent moon","credit":"17th-c. oil, Library of Congress (Kislak), public domain, via Wikimedia Commons"}},{"id":"otumba","t":1520.5137,"name":"Battle of Otompan (Otumba)","kind":"battle","precision":"day","lon":-98.7578,"lat":19.6989,"confidence":"moderate","facts":[["Date (Julian)","7 Jul 1520"],["Gregorian","17 Jul 1520"],["Nahua day (correlation)","2 Calli — a correlation, not an attestation"]],"text":"The retreating column, harried around the lakes' north end, is intercepted on the plain of Otompan by a Mexica-led host and cuts its way through — the cavalry charge that reaches the commander's standard is the day's hinge. The road to Tlaxcallan is open.","accounts":[],"sources":["[C2]","[BD]","[GOM]"],"track":true,"pins":{"[C2]":"Second Letter, the battle on the plain","[BD]":"cap. 128"}},{"id":"tlaxcala-refuge","t":1520.5246,"name":"Tlaxcallan holds: the pact renewed","kind":"political","precision":"day","lon":-98.238,"lat":19.318,"confidence":"moderate","facts":[["Date (Julian)","11 Jul 1520"],["Gregorian","21 Jul 1520"],["Nahua day (correlation)","6 Mázatl — a correlation, not an attestation"]],"text":"The broken company is received, sheltered and re-armed. Tlaxcallan's council debates Mexica peace overtures and refuses them — the war's quietest decisive moment: the coalition survives its defeat.","accounts":[{"source":"Muñoz Camargo; Tlaxcalan tradition","claim":"loyalty freely kept at the hour it mattered, at the price of stated privileges","note":"the petition-era framing of the choice"},{"source":"Mexica overtures per [BD][DUR]","claim":"Cuitláhuac offered Tlaxcallan the partnership the Mexica had always refused","note":"reported through hostile intermediaries; terms unrecoverable"}],"sources":["[BD]","[MC]","[C2]"],"track":false,"pins":null},{"id":"cuitlahuac-tlatoani","t":1520.7077,"name":"Cuitláhuac becomes huey tlatoani","kind":"political","precision":"month","lon":-99.1316,"lat":19.4336,"confidence":"moderate","facts":[["Date (Julian)","16 Sep 1520 (month approximate)"],["Gregorian","26 Sep 1520"]],"text":"Iztapalapan's lord, the war party's head, is elected and crowned. He reopens the causeways, rebuilds alliances where he can, and dies of smallpox after roughly eighty days of a reign spent entirely at war.","accounts":[],"sources":["[FC]","[DUR]","[TH]"],"track":false,"pins":null},{"id":"tepeaca-campaign","t":1520.6749,"name":"The Tepeaca campaign; Segura de la Frontera","kind":"campaign","precision":"day","lon":-97.9,"lat":18.967,"confidence":"moderate","facts":[["Date (Julian)","4 Sep 1520"],["Gregorian","14 Sep 1520"],["Nahua day (correlation)","9 Ehécatl — a correlation, not an attestation"]],"text":"The coalition retakes the eastern road by systematic terror — massacre and enslavement, branding captives with the letter G for guerra. The Spanish town of Segura de la Frontera is founded in Tepeyacac; the corridor to the coast will not close again.","accounts":[],"sources":["[C2]","[BD]","[TH]"],"track":false,"pins":{"[C2]":"Second Letter, the Tepeaca campaign","[BD]":"cap. 130-135"}},{"id":"tepeaca-enslavement","t":1520.7049,"name":"The branding iron at Tepeyacac","kind":"aftermath","precision":"month","lon":-97.9,"lat":18.967,"confidence":"moderate","facts":[["Date (Julian)","15 Sep 1520 (month approximate)"],["Gregorian","25 Sep 1520"]],"text":"In the retaken towns the coalition brands captives — women and children among them — with the letter G, for guerra, and distributes them as slaves. The reprisal economy of the war, recorded without embarrassment in the Spanish accounts themselves.","accounts":[],"sources":["[C2]","[BD]","[TH]"],"track":false,"pins":null},{"id":"cuauhquechollan-defects","t":1520.7869,"name":"Cuauhquechollan turns on its garrison","kind":"political","precision":"month","lon":-98.545,"lat":18.77,"confidence":"moderate","facts":[["Date (Julian)","15 Oct 1520 (month approximate)"],["Gregorian","25 Oct 1520"]],"text":"The altepetl invites the coalition in against the Mexica garrison quartered on it — the pattern, repeated down the road: the empire's own structure supplies the war's manpower. Its lienzo later paints the campaign as its own conquest, shared with the strangers.","accounts":[],"sources":["[C2]","Lienzo de Cuauhquechollan"],"track":false,"pins":null},{"id":"smallpox-basin","t":1520.7486,"name":"Smallpox reaches the Basin","kind":"epidemic","precision":"month","lon":-99.1265,"lat":19.429,"confidence":"contested","facts":[["Date (Julian)","1 Oct 1520 (month approximate)"],["Gregorian","11 Oct 1520"]],"text":"The epidemic that entered at the coast with the Narváez fleet burns through the lake cities for sixty days by the Nahua account — killing, among uncounted others, the huey tlatoani Cuitláhuac and lords in every altepetl, on the defending side of the war only.","accounts":[{"source":"Florentine Codex Bk XII","claim":"sixty days of huey zahuatl in the city; the dead unburied; the living unable to tend the sick","note":"the only inside account of the epidemic in the capital"},{"source":"Motolinía (1541)","claim":"'in most provinces more than half the people died'","note":"a friar's estimate two decades on; the basis of the highest mortality readings"},{"source":"modern demography [CMH]","claim":"Basin mortality in 1520-21 plausibly 30-50%; all precise figures are constructions","note":"the model states a band, never a number"}],"sources":["[FC]","[Motolinía]","[CMH]"],"track":false,"pins":{"[FC]":"Bk XII, ch. 29 — the huey zahuatl"},"image":{"src":"img/cards/fc-smallpox.jpg","caption":"Book XII: a healer tends smallpox victims, drawn by Nahua artists who lived the epidemic's aftermath","credit":"Florentine Codex, Biblioteca Medicea Laurenziana, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons"}},{"id":"cuitlahuac-dies","t":1520.9235,"name":"Cuitláhuac dies of smallpox; Cuauhtémoc chosen","kind":"epidemic","precision":"day","lon":-99.1332,"lat":19.4326,"confidence":"contested","facts":[["Date (Julian)","4 Dec 1520"],["Gregorian","14 Dec 1520"],["Nahua day (correlation)","9 Ácatl — a correlation, not an attestation"]],"text":"The war leader's death after ~80 days leaves the defence to Cuauhtémoc of Tlatelolco — young, implacable, and crowned in a city already burying its leadership class.","accounts":[{"source":"chronology per [TH]","claim":"died 4 December 1520 (some readings: 25 November)","note":"the sources give reign-lengths, not dates; the day is a reconstruction"},{"source":"Florentine Codex Bk XII; Durán","claim":"a reign of eighty days, ended by the huey zahuatl","note":"the Nahua accounts count the reign, not the calendar"}],"sources":["[FC]","[DUR]","[TH]"],"track":false,"pins":null},{"id":"texcoco-taken","t":1520.9973,"name":"The coalition enters Texcoco","kind":"political","precision":"day","lon":-98.883,"lat":19.508,"confidence":"good","facts":[["Date (Julian)","31 Dec 1520"],["Gregorian","10 Jan 1521"],["Nahua day (correlation)","10 Xóchitl — a correlation, not an attestation"]],"text":"Cortés occupies the alliance's second capital unopposed; its Mexica-party lords flee across the lake. Under Ixtlilxóchitl's faction, Acolhua manpower, food and the lakeside shipyard pass to the coalition — the siege becomes materially possible on this day.","accounts":[{"source":"Alva Ixtlilxóchitl","claim":"Ixtlilxóchitl delivered Texcoco and half the empire's manpower to the siege","note":"family chronicle claiming the credit the Spanish accounts withhold"},{"source":"Cortés, Tercera carta","claim":"Texcoco taken by his advance and held by his garrison","note":"allies appear as auxiliaries, never as the reason it worked"}],"sources":["[C3]","[BD]","[IXT]"],"track":true,"pins":{"[C3]":"Third Letter, the occupation of Texcoco","[IXT]":"Historia de la nación chichimeca, cap. 88-90"}},{"id":"iztapalapa-burned","t":1521.0219,"name":"Iztapalapan burned","kind":"battle","precision":"day","lon":-99.092,"lat":19.357,"confidence":"moderate","facts":[["Date (Julian)","9 Jan 1521"],["Gregorian","19 Jan 1521"],["Nahua day (correlation)","6 Atl — a correlation, not an attestation"]],"text":"The coalition's first lake-shore strike from Texcoco: Cuitláhuac's own city, half on the water, is stormed and burned; the defenders open the dike and nearly drown the raiders on the causeway back.","accounts":[],"sources":["[C3]","[BD]"],"track":false,"pins":null},{"id":"chalco-defects","t":1521.0521,"name":"The Chalca towns come over","kind":"political","precision":"month","lon":-98.8975,"lat":19.2647,"confidence":"moderate","facts":[["Date (Julian)","20 Jan 1521 (month approximate)"],["Gregorian","30 Jan 1521"]],"text":"Chalco — conquered by the Mexica within living memory after a generation-long war — asks the coalition for protection against imperial reprisal. The southeast lakeshore, the Basin's granary, changes sides.","accounts":[],"sources":["[C3]","[BD]","Chimalpahin"],"track":false,"pins":{"[C3]":"Third Letter, the Chalca embassies"}},{"id":"xaltocan-circuit","t":1521.1233,"name":"The northern circuit: Xaltocan to Tlacopan","kind":"campaign","precision":"month","lon":-99.089,"lat":19.712,"confidence":"moderate","facts":[["Date (Julian)","15 Feb 1521 (month approximate)"],["Gregorian","25 Feb 1521"]],"text":"A sweep around the northern lakes takes island Xaltocan and burns through Cuauhtitlan, Tenayuca and Azcapotzalco to fight in Tlacopan itself — mapping the ring the siege will close.","accounts":[],"sources":["[C3]","[BD]"],"track":true,"pins":null},{"id":"brigantines-carried","t":1521.137,"name":"A fleet carried over the mountains","kind":"campaign","precision":"month","lon":-98.238,"lat":19.318,"confidence":"moderate","facts":[["Date (Julian)","20 Feb 1521 (month approximate)"],["Gregorian","2 Mar 1521"]],"text":"The thirteen brigantines, prefabricated in Tlaxcallan from the scuttled ships' iron, are carried in pieces over the sierra to Texcoco by a porter column the sources describe in the tens of thousands — the war's plainest demonstration of what allied manpower meant.","accounts":[],"sources":["[C3]","[BD]","[IXT]","[MC]"],"track":false,"pins":null},{"id":"chalco-counterattacks","t":1521.2,"name":"The Mexica strike back at Chalco","kind":"battle","precision":"month","lon":-98.8975,"lat":19.2647,"confidence":"moderate","facts":[["Date (Julian)","15 Mar 1521 (month approximate)"],["Gregorian","25 Mar 1521"]],"text":"Cuauhtémoc's armies come down on the defected Chalca towns again and again through the spring. Chalco sends to Cortés twice saying it cannot hold alone. For some weeks the granary is genuinely back in play — the one stretch of the war where the empire takes ground back.","accounts":[{"source":"Cortés, Third Letter (1522) [C3]","claim":"the Chalca appealed to him as their protector and he despatched relief that settled the matter","note":"the letter is a legal brief for a man who needed to look like a sovereign meeting his obligations"},{"source":"Chimalpahin (Chalca annals, early 17th c.)","claim":"the fighting was Chalco's own war, fought by Chalca lords, the coalition arriving late","note":"written from inside Chalco, to record that the town was an agent and not a prize"}],"sources":["[C3]","[BD]","Chimalpahin"],"track":false,"pins":{"[C3]":"Third Letter, the relief of Chalco"}},{"id":"chalco-relieved","t":1521.2575,"name":"Sandoval's relief: Chalco held","kind":"campaign","precision":"month","lon":-98.8975,"lat":19.2647,"confidence":"moderate","facts":[["Date (Julian)","5 Apr 1521 (month approximate)"],["Gregorian","15 Apr 1521"]],"text":"Gonzalo de Sandoval is sent back to the southeast with a heavy column and the Chalca towns are secured for good. The counter-offensive was the empire's last chance to break the ring before it closed; from here the coalition's line around the lakes is continuous.","accounts":[],"sources":["[C3]","[BD]","Chimalpahin"],"track":false,"pins":{"[C3]":"Third Letter, Sandoval's second march to Chalco"}},{"id":"southern-circuit","t":1521.2795,"name":"The southern circuit: Cuauhnáhuac and Xochimilco","kind":"campaign","precision":"day","lon":-99.2343,"lat":18.9186,"confidence":"moderate","facts":[["Date (Julian)","13 Apr 1521"],["Gregorian","23 Apr 1521"],["Nahua day (correlation)","9 Calli — a correlation, not an attestation"]],"text":"Crossing the sierra, the coalition storms Cuauhnáhuac, then fights two days in Xochimilco's canals — the chinampa city nearly kills Cortés — before circling the lakes home. Every approach to the capital has now been tested.","accounts":[],"sources":["[C3]","[BD]"],"track":true,"pins":null},{"id":"xochimilco-battle","t":1521.2877,"name":"Two days in the canals of Xōchimīlco","kind":"battle","precision":"day","lon":-99.103,"lat":19.2571,"confidence":"moderate","facts":[["Date (Julian)","16 Apr 1521"],["Gregorian","26 Apr 1521"],["Nahua day (correlation)","12 Miquiztli — a correlation, not an attestation"]],"text":"The chinampa city fights the circuit column canal by canal; Cortés is dragged from his horse and nearly taken alive before Tlaxcalteca warriors cut him free. The war's texture at close quarters: causeways, bridges removed, canoes in the gaps.","accounts":[],"sources":["[C3]","[BD]"],"track":false,"pins":null},{"id":"brigantines-launched","t":1521.3205,"name":"Thirteen brigantines launched at Texcoco","kind":"siege","precision":"day","lon":-98.883,"lat":19.508,"confidence":"good","facts":[["Date (Julian)","28 Apr 1521"],["Gregorian","8 May 1521"],["Nahua day (correlation)","11 Técpatl — a correlation, not an attestation"]],"text":"Built of the scuttled fleet's ironwork and Tlaxcalteca-carried timber, assembled by thousands of Acolhua labourers on a dug canal, the lake flotilla launches. Command of the water — the city's food and its escape — is about to change hands.","accounts":[],"sources":["[C3]","[BD]","[IXT]"],"track":false,"pins":{"[C3]":"Third Letter, the launching of the brigantines","[BD]":"cap. 150"}},{"id":"siege-camps","t":1521.3863,"name":"The siege opens: three causeway camps","kind":"siege","precision":"day","lon":-99.134,"lat":19.418,"confidence":"good","facts":[["Date (Julian)","22 May 1521"],["Gregorian","1 Jun 1521"],["Nahua day (correlation)","9 Ehécatl — a correlation, not an attestation"]],"text":"Alvarado takes station at Tlacopan, Olid at Coyoacán, Sandoval at Iztapalapan; the brigantines base at Texcoco. Some 700-900 Spaniards — and, by every account including Cortés's own, tens of thousands of Tlaxcalteca, Acolhua, Chalca and Huexotzinca — invest a city of perhaps 50,000-200,000.","accounts":[],"sources":["[C3]","[BD]","[IXT]"],"track":false,"pins":{"[C3]":"Third Letter, the disposition of the three camps","[BD]":"cap. 150-151"},"image":{"src":"img/cards/siege-painting.jpg","caption":"The assault on the causeway and the temple — painted c. 1696, 175 years after the event and from the victors' side","credit":"'Conquista de México' series, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons"}},{"id":"aqueduct-cut","t":1521.3973,"name":"The Chapultepec aqueduct is cut","kind":"siege","precision":"day","lon":-99.1313,"lat":19.4348,"confidence":"moderate","facts":[["Date (Julian)","26 May 1521"],["Gregorian","5 Jun 1521"],["Nahua day (correlation)","13 Miquiztli — a correlation, not an attestation"]],"text":"The city's fresh water — the twin terracotta channels from the Chapultepec springs — is broken at the source and the ceramic repaired-channel raids beaten off. From this day the defenders drink brackish well water.","accounts":[],"sources":["[C3]","[BD]","[FC]"],"track":false,"pins":{"[C3]":"Third Letter, the cutting of the Chapultepec water"}},{"id":"brigantine-victory","t":1521.4137,"name":"The lake is lost: the canoe fleet broken","kind":"siege","precision":"day","lon":-99.105,"lat":19.43,"confidence":"moderate","facts":[["Date (Julian)","1 Jun 1521"],["Gregorian","11 Jun 1521"],["Nahua day (correlation)","6 Malinalli — a correlation, not an attestation"]],"text":"In the first fleet action the brigantines catch the wind and run down the massed war canoes off Iztapalapan. Supply by water — the island city's logic — is finished; the causeway assaults begin the same week.","accounts":[],"sources":["[C3]","[FC]"],"track":false,"pins":null},{"id":"sandoval-tepeyac","t":1521.4384,"name":"The last road closed: Tepeyacac","kind":"siege","precision":"month","lon":-99.12,"lat":19.468,"confidence":"moderate","facts":[["Date (Julian)","10 Jun 1521 (month approximate)"],["Gregorian","20 Jun 1521"]],"text":"Sandoval is reposted to seal the northern causeway, left open until now as a tempting exit. The ring is complete: six arteries, six tourniquets.","accounts":[],"sources":["[C3]"],"track":false,"pins":null},{"id":"tlatelolco-ambush","t":1521.4932,"name":"The great ambush: the siege's worst Spanish defeat","kind":"battle","precision":"day","lon":-99.1392,"lat":19.4489,"confidence":"moderate","facts":[["Date (Julian)","30 Jun 1521"],["Gregorian","10 Jul 1521"],["Nahua day (correlation)","9 Cipactli — a correlation, not an attestation"]],"text":"A premature push into the city is cut off at a broken causeway gap; 53-68 Spaniards are taken alive (Cortés admits 35-40; Díaz counted 66 plus five more days later) and sacrificed on the pyramid of Tlatelolco in sight of their camps. The coalition's allies waver for days.","accounts":[{"source":"Cortés, Tercera carta","claim":"35-40 taken, through a subordinate's disobedience","note":"minimises a defeat that nearly broke the siege"},{"source":"Bernal Díaz","claim":"66 comrades taken and sacrificed, named and mourned; 72 with the later captures","note":"the eyewitness count from Alvarado's camp"}],"sources":["[C3]","[BD]","[FC]","[AT]"],"track":false,"pins":{"[C3]":"Third Letter, the reverse on the causeway","[BD]":"cap. 152","[FC]":"Bk XII, ch. 34"},"image":{"src":"img/cards/fc-siege.jpg","caption":"Book XII, fol. 67v: captured Spaniards and their allies sacrificed at Colhuacatonco during the siege — the war drawn by the Nahua artists who survived it","credit":"Florentine Codex, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons"}},{"id":"water-famine","t":1521.4959,"name":"Brackish water and the hunger","kind":"siege","precision":"month","lon":-99.1355,"lat":19.452,"confidence":"moderate","facts":[["Date (Julian)","1 Jul 1521 (month approximate)"],["Gregorian","11 Jul 1521"]],"text":"With the aqueduct cut and the lake lost, the city drinks from hastily dug brackish wells and eats, in Book XII's remembering, lizards, swallows, deerhide and adobe scrapings. The siege's principal weapon is doing its work; no assault kills as many.","accounts":[],"sources":["[FC]","[AT]","[C3]"],"track":false,"pins":null},{"id":"malinalco-expedition","t":1521.5205,"name":"The flanks secured: Malinalco and the Matlatzinca","kind":"campaign","precision":"month","lon":-99.494,"lat":18.948,"confidence":"moderate","facts":[["Date (Julian)","10 Jul 1521 (month approximate)"],["Gregorian","20 Jul 1521"]],"text":"Mexica attempts to raise the southwest against the besiegers' rear are broken by detached columns at Malinalco and in the Toluca valley.","accounts":[],"sources":["[C3]","[BD]"],"track":false,"pins":null},{"id":"razing-advance","t":1521.5479,"name":"The city unmade street by street","kind":"siege","precision":"month","lon":-99.133,"lat":19.431,"confidence":"good","facts":[["Date (Julian)","20 Jul 1521 (month approximate)"],["Gregorian","30 Jul 1521"]],"text":"After the ambush the siege changes method: every canal filled, every building pulled down as the lines advance, the rubble making the causeway the attackers need. The Mexica defence compresses into Tlatelolco with the famine and the wells.","accounts":[],"sources":["[C3]","[FC]","[AT]"],"track":false,"pins":null},{"id":"surrender-refused","t":1521.5616,"name":"The offers refused","kind":"political","precision":"month","lon":-99.139,"lat":19.4532,"confidence":"contested","facts":[["Date (Julian)","25 Jul 1521 (month approximate)"],["Gregorian","4 Aug 1521"]],"text":"Between assaults, terms pass across the rubble — and are refused. What was actually offered, and by whom, each side reports to its own advantage; what is certain is that the defence chose to continue.","accounts":[{"source":"Cortés, Tercera carta","claim":"repeated generous offers of peace, each spurned by Cuauhtémoc's obstinacy","note":"mercy offered and refused is the conqueror's absolution for what follows"},{"source":"Florentine Codex; Anales de Tlatelolco","claim":"the demands amounted to surrender of persons, gods and rule — no terms a tlatoani could take","note":"the defence's arithmetic: annihilation fighting, or annihilation yielding"}],"sources":["[C3]","[FC]","[AT]"],"track":false,"pins":null},{"id":"tlatelolco-market-fall","t":1521.5808,"name":"The last stand compresses into the market","kind":"siege","precision":"month","lon":-99.1352,"lat":19.4505,"confidence":"good","facts":[["Date (Julian)","1 Aug 1521 (month approximate)"],["Gregorian","11 Aug 1521"]],"text":"The defence, starving, holds a shrinking quarter around the great market of Tlatelolco. The great pyramid burns; the war songs later collected from the survivors date themselves to these days: 'broken spears lie in the roads.'","accounts":[],"sources":["[FC]","[AT]","[C3]"],"track":false,"pins":null},{"id":"fall-tenochtitlan","t":1521.6137,"name":"The fall: Cuauhtémoc taken on the water","kind":"siege","precision":"day","lon":-99.124,"lat":19.447,"confidence":"good","facts":[["Date (Julian)","13 Aug 1521"],["Gregorian","23 Aug 1521"],["Nahua day (correlation)","1 Cóatl — a correlation, not an attestation"]],"text":"On the day 1 Cóatl, with the defence starved into a corner of Tlatelolco, Cuauhtémoc is captured by brigantine while crossing the lake. The Mexica state ends; the sack that follows is remembered in Nahua song as the broken spears and the walls red. Roughly 40,000-100,000 defenders and civilians died in the siege by varying accounts; no source counted the allies' dead.","accounts":[],"sources":["[C3]","[BD]","[FC]","[AT]"],"track":false,"pins":{"[C3]":"Third Letter, the taking of Cuauhtémoc","[BD]":"cap. 156","[FC]":"Bk XII, ch. 40-41","[AT]":"f. 39"},"image":{"src":"img/cards/map-1524.jpg","caption":"The 1524 Nuremberg woodcut: Tenochtitlan as Europe first saw it — schematic (best-fit residual 2.2 km), west at top","credit":"Newberry Library scan, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons"}},{"id":"cuauhtemoc-tortured","t":1521.6247,"name":"Cuauhtémoc tortured for the treasure","kind":"aftermath","precision":"month","lon":-99.1617,"lat":19.3467,"confidence":"moderate","facts":[["Date (Julian)","17 Aug 1521 (month approximate)"],["Gregorian","27 Aug 1521"]],"text":"At the Coyoacán headquarters the royal treasurer has Cuauhtémoc's feet burned to make him yield the gold lost on the Noche Triste. Cortés permits it; the gold is mostly not found.","accounts":[{"source":"Bernal Díaz; Gómara","claim":"the torture happened and shamed those who allowed it","note":"even the conquerors' own accounts record it"},{"source":"Cortés, Cartas","claim":"passes over the episode","note":"silence in a legal self-portrait is itself information"}],"sources":["[BD]","[GOM]"],"track":false,"pins":null},{"id":"slaves-branded","t":1521.7041,"name":"The distribution of persons","kind":"aftermath","precision":"month","lon":-99.1617,"lat":19.3467,"confidence":"moderate","facts":[["Date (Julian)","15 Sep 1521 (month approximate)"],["Gregorian","25 Sep 1521"]],"text":"Captives of the siege are branded and allotted; the crown's fifth is taken in people as well as gold. The coalition's indigenous soldiers watch their share of the victory assessed at less than they were promised — the first lesson in what the new order weighs.","accounts":[],"sources":["[C3]","[BD]","[GIB]"],"track":false,"pins":null},{"id":"coyoacan-banquet","t":1521.7863,"name":"The victory banquet at Coyohuacan","kind":"aftermath","precision":"month","lon":-99.1617,"lat":19.3467,"confidence":"moderate","facts":[["Date (Julian)","15 Oct 1521 (month approximate)"],["Gregorian","25 Oct 1521"]],"text":"Wine from a ship at the coast; a banquet for the conquerors that ends in drunken scandal Bernal Díaz thought shameful enough to record. Beside it, in the same weeks: the treasure counted, and found a fraction of what the Noche Triste swallowed.","accounts":[],"sources":["[BD]"],"track":false,"pins":null},{"id":"oaxaca-taken","t":1521.9534,"name":"The Oaxaca valley occupied","kind":"aftermath","precision":"month","lon":-96.782,"lat":16.997,"confidence":"moderate","facts":[["Date (Julian)","15 Dec 1521 (month approximate)"],["Gregorian","25 Dec 1521"]],"text":"Orozco's column takes the Valley of Oaxaca garrisons; the southern tribute provinces pass to the new regime with little fighting.","accounts":[],"sources":["[GER]","[HAS]"],"track":false,"pins":null},{"id":"rebuilding-begins","t":1522.0384,"name":"Mexico City rises on the razed grid","kind":"aftermath","precision":"month","lon":-99.1325,"lat":19.4335,"confidence":"good","facts":[["Date (Julian)","15 Jan 1522 (month approximate)"],["Gregorian","25 Jan 1522"]],"text":"The city is rebuilt as the Spanish capital on Tenochtitlan's own plan — the traza for the conquerors at the centre, the surviving Mexica moved to the parcialidades at the edges, native labour and the temple stones doing the building. San Francisco rises where the aviary stood.","accounts":[],"sources":["[GIB]","[TH]"],"track":false,"pins":null},{"id":"tototepec-taken","t":1522.1616,"name":"Alvarado takes Tototepec","kind":"aftermath","precision":"month","lon":-97.605,"lat":16.132,"confidence":"moderate","facts":[["Date (Julian)","1 Mar 1522 (month approximate)"],["Gregorian","11 Mar 1522"]],"text":"The coastal Mixtec kingdom that the Triple Alliance never subdued falls to a Spanish-led column in weeks — then rises in 1523 against the occupation's exactions, and is broken again.","accounts":[],"sources":["[GER]","[HAS]"],"track":false,"pins":null},{"id":"michoacan-submits","t":1522.5342,"name":"The Purépecha state submits without a siege","kind":"aftermath","precision":"month","lon":-101.579,"lat":19.628,"confidence":"moderate","facts":[["Date (Julian)","15 Jul 1522 (month approximate)"],["Gregorian","25 Jul 1522"]],"text":"The cazonci Tangáxuan, whose armies broke Axayácatl's invasion in the 1470s, watches the greater empire die of siege and plague, and receives Olid's column at Tzintzuntzan without battle — the other empire ends by calculation, not conquest.","accounts":[],"sources":["[GER]","Relación de Michoacán (1541)"],"track":false,"pins":null},{"id":"cortes-governor","t":1522.7863,"name":"The crown legalises the fact: Cortés governor","kind":"political","precision":"day","lon":-99.129,"lat":19.433,"confidence":"good","facts":[["Date (Julian)","15 Oct 1522"],["Gregorian","25 Oct 1522"],["Nahua day (correlation)","13 Ácatl — a correlation, not an attestation"]],"text":"Charles V names Cortés governor and captain-general of 'New Spain' — the retroactive blessing the Cartas were written to obtain. Mexico City is already rising on Tenochtitlan's razed grid.","accounts":[],"sources":["royal cédula 15 Oct 1522","[TH]"],"track":false,"pins":null},{"id":"panuco-campaign","t":1523.0384,"name":"The Pánuco campaign","kind":"aftermath","precision":"month","lon":-97.408,"lat":20.955,"confidence":"moderate","facts":[["Date (Julian)","15 Jan 1523 (month approximate)"],["Gregorian","25 Jan 1523"]],"text":"Cortés subdues the Huasteca in a winter campaign of massacre and burning; the northeastern provinces are folded into New Spain.","accounts":[],"sources":["[C4]","[GER]"],"track":false,"pins":null},{"id":"guerrero-coast","t":1523.4137,"name":"The southern coast reduced","kind":"aftermath","precision":"year","lon":-101.55,"lat":17.64,"confidence":"moderate","facts":[["Date (Julian)","1 Jun 1523 (year-level date)"],["Gregorian","11 Jun 1523"]],"text":"Columns through Guerrero bring the Pacific tribute coast and the Tlapanec highlands under the new regime.","accounts":[],"sources":["[GER]"],"track":false,"pins":null},{"id":"soconusco-consolidated","t":1524.0,"name":"Soconusco and the far provinces absorbed","kind":"aftermath","precision":"year","lon":-92.6,"lat":15.0,"confidence":"moderate","facts":[["Date (Julian)","1 Jan 1524 (year-level date)"],["Gregorian","11 Jan 1524"]],"text":"The cacao coast passes under Spanish control as Alvarado's Guatemala expedition marches through and beyond it.","accounts":[],"sources":["[GER]"],"track":false,"pins":null},{"id":"franciscan-twelve","t":1524.4153,"name":"The Twelve arrive: the spiritual conquest begins","kind":"religious","precision":"month","lon":-99.1391,"lat":19.4339,"confidence":"good","facts":[["Date (Julian)","1 Jun 1524 (month approximate)"],["Gregorian","11 Jun 1524"]],"text":"Twelve Franciscans walk barefoot from the coast; Cortés kneels to them before the assembled lords. The systematic replacement of the Mexica sacred order — temple demolition, the schools, the burnings of the books — begins.","accounts":[],"sources":["[Motolinía]","Sahagún, Coloquios","[GIB]"],"track":false,"pins":null},{"id":"honduras-march","t":1524.7787,"name":"The Honduras march leaves the valley","kind":"campaign","precision":"day","lon":-99.1313,"lat":19.4348,"confidence":"good","facts":[["Date (Julian)","12 Oct 1524"],["Gregorian","22 Oct 1524"],["Nahua day (correlation)","13 Cipactli — a correlation, not an attestation"]],"text":"Cortés marches for Honduras against a mutinous lieutenant, taking the captive lords — Cuauhtémoc among them — because leaving them behind is judged more dangerous. Two years of jungle catastrophe follow; the expedition accomplishes nothing and kills the last huey tlatoani.","accounts":[],"sources":["[C5]","[BD]","[TH]"],"track":false,"pins":null},{"id":"cuauhtemoc-executed","t":1525.1589,"name":"Cuauhtémoc hanged at Itzamkánac","kind":"aftermath","precision":"day","lon":-90.85,"lat":17.85,"confidence":"good","facts":[["Date (Julian)","28 Feb 1525"],["Gregorian","10 Mar 1525"],["Nahua day (correlation)","9 Xóchitl — a correlation, not an attestation"]],"text":"On the Honduras march, on an informer's word of conspiracy, Cortés hangs the last huey tlatoani and the lords of Tlacopan and Texcoco from a ceiba far from home. Even Spaniards on the march called it murder.","accounts":[{"source":"Cortés, Quinta carta","claim":"a confessed conspiracy justified summary justice","note":"the executioner's account of the necessity"},{"source":"Bernal Díaz","claim":"'it was a most unjust thing and appeared wrong to all of us'","note":"a participant's verdict written into the soldiers' history"}],"sources":["[BD]","[C5]","[TH]"],"track":false,"pins":{"[BD]":"cap. 177"}},{"id":"first-audiencia","t":1528.9372,"name":"The first audiencia: government as plunder","kind":"political","precision":"month","lon":-99.1313,"lat":19.4348,"confidence":"good","facts":[["Date (Julian)","9 Dec 1528 (month approximate)"],["Gregorian","19 Dec 1528"]],"text":"The crown's first governing board arrives under Nuño de Guzmán and makes rapacity systematic — encomiendas reshuffled to friends, native lords shaken down, the conquerors' own chronicles appalled. Royal government has arrived; good government has not.","accounts":[],"sources":["[GIB]","[TH]"],"track":false,"pins":null},{"id":"zumarraga-bishop","t":1528.9536,"name":"Zumárraga, first bishop and 'Protector of the Indians'","kind":"religious","precision":"month","lon":-99.1313,"lat":19.4348,"confidence":"good","facts":[["Date (Julian)","15 Dec 1528 (month approximate)"],["Gregorian","25 Dec 1528"]],"text":"The Franciscan Juan de Zumárraga arrives as bishop-elect with a protector's title and a reformer's mandate he cannot yet enforce against the audiencia. The church becomes, unevenly, both shelter and instrument.","accounts":[],"sources":["[GIB]"],"track":false,"pins":null},{"id":"guzman-west","t":1529.9671,"name":"Nuño de Guzmán's western march; the cazonci burned","kind":"aftermath","precision":"month","lon":-101.579,"lat":19.628,"confidence":"good","facts":[["Date (Julian)","20 Dec 1529 (month approximate)"],["Gregorian","30 Dec 1529"]],"text":"The first audiencia's president marches west with an army of Mexica and Tlaxcalteca auxiliaries, tortures and burns the Purépecha cazonci Tangáxuan, and devastates the west — colonial violence exceeding the conquest's, under legal forms.","accounts":[{"source":"Guzmán's own proceso (1530)","claim":"the cazonci tried and executed for treason, idolatry and withholding tribute","note":"a legal record produced by the man who profited"},{"source":"Relación de Michoacán (1541); later royal inquiry","claim":"judicial murder for gold; Guzmán was eventually recalled in chains","note":"the crown's own reckoning, a decade late"}],"sources":["trial records","[GER]","Relación de Michoacán"],"track":false,"pins":null},{"id":"second-audiencia","t":1531.0247,"name":"The second audiencia begins the repair","kind":"political","precision":"month","lon":-99.1313,"lat":19.4348,"confidence":"good","facts":[["Date (Julian)","10 Jan 1531 (month approximate)"],["Gregorian","20 Jan 1531"]],"text":"Under Ramírez de Fuenleal and Vasco de Quiroga the replacement board reverses Guzmán's grants, hears native suits, and begins building the legal order that royal government will inherit.","accounts":[],"sources":["[GIB]"],"track":false,"pins":null},{"id":"yope-war","t":1531.4137,"name":"The Yope rising crushed","kind":"aftermath","precision":"year","lon":-98.95,"lat":16.95,"confidence":"moderate","facts":[["Date (Julian)","1 Jun 1531 (year-level date)"],["Gregorian","11 Jun 1531"]],"text":"The Yope enclave — never conquered by the Triple Alliance — rises against tribute and encomienda and is destroyed as a polity; the last unconquered pocket of the old map is gone.","accounts":[],"sources":["[GER]"],"track":false,"pins":null},{"id":"guadalupe-tradition","t":1531.937,"name":"Tepeyacac: the apparition tradition","kind":"religious","precision":"day","lon":-99.1172,"lat":19.4847,"confidence":"contested","facts":[["Date (Julian)","9 Dec 1531"],["Gregorian","19 Dec 1531"],["Nahua day (correlation)","1 Cuauhtli — a correlation, not an attestation"]],"text":"Later tradition places the Virgin's apparitions to Juan Diego at Tepeyacac — where the causeway met the shrine of the mother-goddess Tonantzin — in December 1531. The event's date belongs to the tradition; the earliest surviving narrative is more than a century younger, and the devotion's growth is itself part of this model's aftermath.","accounts":[{"source":"the Guadalupan tradition (Nican Mopohua, pub. 1649)","claim":"the Virgin appeared to Juan Diego at Tepeyacac, 9-12 December 1531, leaving her image on his tilma","note":"the founding narrative of Mexico's central devotion, first printed 118 years after the date it records"},{"source":"the documentary record of the 1530s-40s","claim":"contemporary sources, including Zumárraga's papers, are silent; Sahagún later worried the Tepeyacac cult continued Tonantzin's under a new name","note":"silence is not disproof; the model dates the TRADITION and says which is which"}],"sources":["Nican Mopohua (pub. 1649)","Sahagún (on Tonantzin/Tepeyacac)","[GIB]"],"track":false,"pins":null},{"id":"mendoza-viceroy","t":1535.8685,"name":"Antonio de Mendoza, first viceroy","kind":"political","precision":"day","lon":-99.1286,"lat":19.4336,"confidence":"good","facts":[["Date (Julian)","14 Nov 1535"],["Gregorian","24 Nov 1535"],["Nahua day (correlation)","7 Ozomahtli — a correlation, not an attestation"]],"text":"Royal government arrives in the person of a viceroy; the conquerors' improvised regime gives way to bureaucratic empire. It is for Mendoza that the codex bearing his name is painted.","accounts":[],"sources":["[GIB]","[TH]"],"track":false,"pins":null},{"id":"colegio-tlatelolco","t":1536.0137,"name":"The Colegio de Santa Cruz opens at Tlatelolco","kind":"religious","precision":"day","lon":-99.1378,"lat":19.451,"confidence":"good","facts":[["Date (Julian)","6 Jan 1536"],["Gregorian","16 Jan 1536"],["Nahua day (correlation)","8 Cuetzpalin — a correlation, not an attestation"]],"text":"A college for the sons of the Nahua nobility: Latin, rhetoric, medicine, and the scriptorium where, a generation on, Sahagún's students will write down the elders' memory of this whole war — the institution that makes Book XII possible.","accounts":[],"sources":["[GIB]","SilverMoon (2007)"],"track":false,"pins":null},{"id":"printing-press","t":1539.4137,"name":"The first printing press in the Americas","kind":"political","precision":"year","lon":-99.1345,"lat":19.4332,"confidence":"good","facts":[["Date (Julian)","1 Jun 1539 (year-level date)"],["Gregorian","11 Jun 1539"]],"text":"Juan Pablos sets up Cromberger's press in Mexico City; its first productions are doctrinas in Nahuatl — the conquerors' faith, set in the conquered language, on the hemisphere's first press.","accounts":[],"sources":["[GIB]"],"track":false,"pins":null},{"id":"don-carlos-executed","t":1539.9123,"name":"Don Carlos of Texcoco burned for heresy","kind":"religious","precision":"day","lon":-98.883,"lat":19.508,"confidence":"good","facts":[["Date (Julian)","30 Nov 1539"],["Gregorian","10 Dec 1539"],["Nahua day (correlation)","2 Tochtli — a correlation, not an attestation"]],"text":"Zumárraga's inquisition tries don Carlos Ometochtzin, lord of Texcoco and grandson of Nezahualcóyotl, for concealed rites and defiant words, and burns him at the stake. The scandal reaches Spain; native converts are afterwards removed from the Inquisition's full reach — one pyre too visible even for the machinery that lit it.","accounts":[],"sources":["trial record (proceso, 1539)","[GIB]"],"track":false,"pins":null},{"id":"codex-mendoza-made","t":1541.4137,"name":"The Codex Mendoza is painted","kind":"political","precision":"year","lon":-99.133,"lat":19.4346,"confidence":"moderate","facts":[["Date (Julian)","1 Jun 1541 (year-level date)"],["Gregorian","11 Jun 1541"]],"text":"At the viceroy's commission, Nahua tlacuiloque paint the empire for the emperor across the sea: the conquests of each ruler, the tribute of each province, a life from cradle to old age — the very document this model's gazetteer leans on, made twenty years after the fall by the system's own scribes.","accounts":[],"sources":["[CM] Berdan & Anawalt (1992)"],"track":false,"pins":{"[CM] Berdan & Anawalt (1992)":"fol. 1r-71v — the tribute roll itself"},"image":{"src":"img/cards/mendoza-tribute.jpg","caption":"A Codex Mendoza tribute folio: town glyphs and their semi-annual dues in cloth, feathers and stone","credit":"Bodleian Library, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons"}},{"id":"mixton-war","t":1541.6658,"name":"The Mixtón war","kind":"aftermath","precision":"month","lon":-103.0,"lat":21.05,"confidence":"good","facts":[["Date (Julian)","1 Sep 1541 (month approximate)"],["Gregorian","11 Sep 1541"]],"text":"The peoples of the northwest rise against encomienda and slaving raids and besiege Guadalajara; Mendoza takes the field with tens of thousands of Mexica and Tlaxcalteca troops. Native New Spain fights on both sides of every colonial war.","accounts":[],"sources":["[GIB]","[GER]"],"track":false,"pins":null},{"id":"new-laws","t":1542.8849,"name":"The New Laws: encomienda checked on paper","kind":"political","precision":"day","lon":-99.1313,"lat":19.4348,"confidence":"good","facts":[["Date (Julian)","20 Nov 1542"],["Gregorian","30 Nov 1542"],["Nahua day (correlation)","9 Océlotl — a correlation, not an attestation"]],"text":"Charles V's New Laws bar new encomiendas, free enslaved natives, and order existing grants to lapse — Las Casas's argument become statute. In Peru the conquerors revolt; in New Spain the viceroy suspends the sharpest articles. The law and the fact begin their long negotiation.","accounts":[],"sources":["the ordinances (20 Nov 1542)","[GIB]"],"track":false,"pins":null},{"id":"cocoliztli-1545","t":1545.4137,"name":"The great cocoliztli","kind":"epidemic","precision":"year","lon":-99.136,"lat":19.43,"confidence":"contested","facts":[["Date (Julian)","1 Jun 1545 (year-level date)"],["Gregorian","11 Jun 1545"]],"text":"An epidemic the sources call cocoliztli — its identity still debated — kills on a scale that dwarfs 1520: estimates run from 800,000 into the millions across New Spain. The demographic catastrophe of the century is not the siege but the diseases.","accounts":[{"source":"colonial counts and chronicles","claim":"deaths in the hundreds of thousands to millions, 1545-48","note":"no reliable base population; every figure is a construction"},{"source":"modern demography [CMH]","claim":"central Mexico's population fell by perhaps 80-90% across the 16th century, epidemics the largest cause","note":"the band is wide and the model draws it wide"}],"sources":["[Motolinía]","[CMH]"],"track":false,"pins":null},{"id":"sahagun-begins","t":1547.4137,"name":"Sahagún begins collecting the elders' testimony","kind":"religious","precision":"year","lon":-99.1364,"lat":19.4506,"confidence":"good","facts":[["Date (Julian)","1 Jun 1547 (year-level date)"],["Gregorian","11 Jun 1547"]],"text":"Bernardino de Sahagún, with his Nahua students of the Colegio, begins the systematic interviews that will grow into the Florentine Codex — including Book XII, this war as the Tlatelolca elders remembered it. The source this model quotes is being created inside the model's own window.","accounts":[],"sources":["Sahagún, prologues","[GIB]"],"track":false,"pins":null},{"id":"congregacion-1550","t":1550.0,"name":"Congregación: the map itself is remade","kind":"aftermath","precision":"year","lon":-99.126,"lat":19.438,"confidence":"good","facts":[["Date (Julian)","1 Jan 1550 (year-level date)"],["Gregorian","11 Jan 1550"]],"text":"By mid-century the crown is gathering the survivors of dispersed altepetl into planned towns; the lakes are shrinking under drainage and the chinampa economy contracting. The world the model opened with — lake cities in a tributary web — no longer exists to map.","accounts":[],"sources":["[GIB]","[GER]"],"track":false,"pins":null,"image":{"src":"img/cards/uppsala-map.jpg","caption":"The Uppsala map, c. 1550: Mexico City drawn by Nahua hands a generation after the fall — the world this model ends in","credit":"Mapa Uppsala (c. 1550), public domain, via Wikimedia Commons"}}];
